item.content:encoded:
Paper by <a href="http://eps.berkeley.edu/people/william-boos">Bill Boos</a> here in Berkeley. I find it very interesting because it basically takes the approach that Chadwick and I discarded when thinking about <span style="color: #0000ff">future tropical precipitation change</span>. They use the divergence of surface energy to explain changes in mid-Holocene rainfall. The model is that forcing absorbed by the ocean stays there and forcing absorbed by the land is re-distributed. <em>I.e. Lambert et al., (2011)?</em> An extract from an email I sent to Bill sums it up:
<blockquote><span style="color: #333399"><em>"It is very interesting how a very different, separate literature around future climate change has grown up independently of what you and colleagues have been saying about paleo. I don't suppose you've tried this for future global warming? I'm guessing not, or it didn't work? The reason I say that is that I looked into using some kind of radiative forcing induced energy transport to explain tropical precipitation changes to very different 2xCO2 and +4K SST forcing runs some years ago. Much to my disappointment, the changes attributable to forcing are small compared with changes related to changes in SST and SST patterns. After my study, a debate between Sandrine Bony and Rob Chadwick emerged on the same question. Chadwick was victorious with SST again carrying the day... In the paleo case, however, the forcing is very large I think you say, but because it's a redistribution of forcing the changes in surface temperatures are not particularly big(???) meaning the forcing driven changes dominate allowing you to use atmospheric heat transport divergence to solve for precipitation change. That's my guess anyway and I'd be interested to hear what you think. I think there is the potential for the perspectives to be unified, however, assuming I haven't really misunderstood what is going on."</em></span></blockquote>
&nbsp;
